Ladybird matches the parts of a “transition” value against
transition-property last, so a keyword the easing function can take
goes to it, as section 4.2 of CSS Values 4 says for a <custom-ident>.
Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 agree with Ladybird on all 22
lines of the test: every easing keyword on its own, steps(),
cubic-bezier() and linear() functions, none, all, “ease ease”, a
comma-separated list, allow-discrete, two times, the “ease-in
ease-out” animation from CSS Values 4’s own example, and that
“transition: 0.15s ease” starts a transition when the width changes.
All three also serialize “transition: ease 1s” as “1s”, as Ladybird now
does, where it gave “ease 1s” before.

All three parse the shorthand in that order. Blink and WebKit each
match it against a transitionShorthandForParsing() that lists
transition-property after the duration, timing function, delay and
behavior, and Gecko’s parse_one_transition() tries the property only
after those.
